      "utf8": "diluted away a little less quickly than the universe expands."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351549,
    "dDurationMs": 8231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, if the parameter is below -1/3 but larger than -1, that's still accelerating expansion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359780,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in that case the dark energy is decreasing over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363060,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The popular idea for that scenario is called quintessence and it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367039,
    "dDurationMs": 4821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something for a future episode. For today, let's push things to the limit:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371860,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if we make the equation of state parameter smaller than negative one?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375940,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How about -1.5; -3; -1,000,000 ?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380500,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- that's the case where the density doesn't just stay constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383540,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the universe expands, but it actually increases. The result would be that the acceleration increases over time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390680,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offhand, that doesn't sound so much crazier than regular old dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395860,
    "dDurationMs": 8300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "except that it is much crazier. As the rate of expansion increases and with no gravitational bodies left"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404160,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to resist the expansion, all points in space will eventually be racing apart from each other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409900,
    "dDurationMs": 1827,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faster than the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411727,
    "dDurationMs": 6853,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That faster than light recession of space is already happening but right now four patches of space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418580,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very far apart - like, many billions of light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422800,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If two patches of space are moving away from each other, faster than light, then they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427980,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can never communicate with each other - the distance from us to the nearest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432800,
    "dDurationMs": 8140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inaccessible region of space is called the 'cosmic event horizon'. Now, if the expansion is accelerating,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440940,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then, over time the distance between patches of lightspeed space gets smaller"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446220,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that is actually happening - your cosmic event horizon gets closer and closer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452060,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually, we won't even be able to see the nearest galaxies because they'll be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455460,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving away too quickly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458480,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as long as we have a nice gravitationally bound galaxy to live in,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462180,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cosmic event horizon can never shrink to a size smaller than that galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467500,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's not the case if dark energy increases. After our galaxy is disrupted by the increasing dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's no protection from the encroaching cosmic event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480060,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The final result is that no particles will be close enough to interact with each other, ever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486260,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, protons and neutrons will be separated into their component quarks..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490520,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is the big room scenario, It happens when the cosmic event horizon is smaller"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495920,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the smallest possible structure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What could cause something like this? No idea. No one has any idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505720,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That doesn't stop us from giving it a name, we call any dark energy that increases in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510620,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strength - so with an equation of state less than -1, 'phantom energy'."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517490,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That name seems to have been coined by Dartmouth physicist Robert Caldwell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522180,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a seminal 2002 paper, \"The Phantom Menace\" - and that was three years after the theatrical release"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529800,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and I guess we still thought it was a sign of the end of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let's see what this made up math has to say about when the big rip would happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539180,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any equation of state parameter smaller than -1 means a big rip."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543500,
    "dDurationMs": 8020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The smaller the parameter, the sooner it happens. Caldwell does this calculation for the case of W=-1.5"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551520,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this scenario, the big rip happens in twenty two billion years from now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things don't get really messy until near the end, around a billion years before the big rip,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561000,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy clusters are ripped apart. At 60 million years and counting, the Milky Way is shredded."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566820,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, this doesn't mean that the cosmic event horizon is quite inside the Milky Way just yet,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572780,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just that the effect of phantom energy is stronger than the gravity binding the stars together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578260,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, at this point the cosmic event horizon is still about 200 million light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584660,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so there should be a handful of galaxies still visible to us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588480,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are some millions of years of fun as we watch those galaxies disassemble"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593900,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the constellations of stars in the Milky Way fly apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598500,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The final deadly stage only happens in the last month or so when the solar system is pulled apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603780,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the last 30 minutes phantom energy is strong enough to overcome Earth's own gravitational binding energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the planet is disrupted. Moments later at the 10^-19 of a second,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617160,
    "dDurationMs": 7220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before absolute disruption, it will overcome all chemical bonds then the forces binding atoms together,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624380,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then nucleons, and then presumably anything smaller. In its final state a big rip"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630320,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe will be nothing but hopelessly isolated elementary particles separated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635660,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by infinitely expanding space. That's a hell of a story."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639940,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How sure can we be that it won't happen? well, somewhat - mostly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645960,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our efforts to understand the nature of dark energy are focused on measuring the value"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650720,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this 'equation of state parameter thing'."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can do that by combining all of the clues to dark energy's behavior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658080,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, in the patterns of the Cosmic Microwave Background,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the baryon acoustic oscillations and in supernovae - We've talked about all of these thing before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668040,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Combined with a few other measures these tell us that the equation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671560,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of state of dark energy is very very close to -1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676340,
    "dDurationMs": 8100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The plunk science team calculate -1.028, plus or minus, 0.032 -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684500,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Phantom energy is still a faint possibility but even at its most extreme"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689500,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the planck estimate, the big rip would be around 75 billion years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695220,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time to get our affairs in order. But more likely is that W is exactly -1,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning dark energy is constant for three reasons:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706980,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first, it seems too much of a coincidence that it should be so close to negative one without being negative one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714540,
    "dDurationMs": 6820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Second, there's a plausible physical explanation for a constant dark energy that the vacuum has a set amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721360,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of energy per volume. There are at least some physical ideas for that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As far as I know, there aren't serious ideas for an increasing dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731620,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the final reason to hate on phantom energy: it violates energy conservation in a way far worse than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738860,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regular dark energy. It violates the same energy conditions of general relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745000,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that prohibit negative mass and time travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747720,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, so, after all that hate let's finally get to the one significant result that suggests"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753300,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark energy may be increasing after all. In fact, we've already covered it in our recent journal Club episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760000,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The acceleration rate measured using distant quasars hints at an equation of state parameter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766020,
    "dDurationMs": 7500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slightly less than negative 1 well they're not yet with enough significance to overturn all other results."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773520,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably, probably - the universe will still end in a long cold heat death"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779300,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the stars of our galaxy wink out become black holes and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782320,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then evaporate over an unthinkably long future. But maybe if you believe in phantoms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788860,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it'll all be over much sooner when the universe is ripped to shreds by the infinitely accelerating subatomic structure of space-time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798540,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we did a spacetime journal Club on that paper that actually presented evidence that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803420,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark energy may be increasing in strength based on measurements of distant quasars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808060,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lots of fun discussion ensued. A few of you asked a great question:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812380,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"What happens when the big rip tries to separate quark pairs?\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817580,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For background, when you try to separate composite quark particles - hadrons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821690,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the energy put into breaking the bond just generates new quarks - so you just get more hadrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828820,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dot follows up by answering the question with a detailed internet search. Meaning, I don't have to answer it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835300,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks Dot! ...but to summarize:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838540,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is: maybe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840120,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Depends on whether the splitting hadrons can keep up with the exponentially increasing dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845340,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The final possibility that Dot found is the most fun - that the exponentially increasing dark energy leads to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850880,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exponential particle production, which ends up looking like a new Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856200,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds cool, but be cautious of theories that seem crafted to give happy endings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861620,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We should do a statistical analysis of cosmology papers that predict saving the universe versus it dying horribly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868480,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'm guessing the save the universe theories are more numerous than their plausibility warrants."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874420,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tony Stark asks, \"if dark energy changed in the past, can it also change in the future?\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880760,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, if it changed in the past then we expect it to change in the future - at least according to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886460,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the standard/non-standard models of changing dark energy. It could be increasing in the future,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892780,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's phantom energy or it could be decreasing. But even if dark energy is decreasing and decreases to zero -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899460,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fact even if it became zero tomorrow, the universe is now expanding too fast to ever recollapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906160,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The amount of mass and energy it contains just doesn't provide enough gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910620,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, if dark energy evolved into an attractive rather than repulsive force, who knows!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916220,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's no good reason to believe that it will but we also don't know what dark energy even is - so, live in hope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923540,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Onehit Pick notes that dark energy backs up their hypothesis that massive dinosaur fossils were actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929520,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "originally chicken sized. The tiny bird bones grew with the expansion of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934720,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Checkmate, evolutionists. They would also explain how they fit elephants on the ark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940620,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and those extra pounds I put on over the winter."
    } ]
  } ]
}
